<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Krishna</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Vasudeva-Krishna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Krishna is one of the most widely worshipped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> gods, revered as an avatar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> or as the supreme deity. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> he is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadava</a> prince who counsels <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a>, and later texts celebrate his childhood among the cowherds around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a>. His cult, early known as that of Vasudeva-Krishna, inspired devotional poetry, temples and painting across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/krishna.jpg" alt="Krishna" width="320" height="467" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Miskin · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Krishna_Holds_Up_Mount_Govardhan_(MET_70694)_cropped.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
